<section class="research-section" id="spectrum-sharing">
  <h2>Bi-Criteria Spectrum Sharing</h2>
  <p>When two networks share a band, there is no single objective to maximize: each has its own throughput, and improving one generally costs the other. Writing the two criteria as \(f_1, f_2 : X \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}\) over a shared parameter vector \(x\), the trade-off is captured by the convex scalarization</p>
  <p>\[ g(x;\lambda) = \lambda f_1(x) + (1-\lambda) f_2(x), \qquad \lambda \in [0,1], \]</p>
  <p>whose maximizers \(x^*(\lambda)\) sweep the Pareto set as \(\lambda\) varies. Solving this independently on a grid of weights is wasteful and produces a disconnected scatter of points rather than a curve.</p>

  <h3>Tracing rather than re-solving</h3>
  <p>The key move is to treat \(x^*\) as a <em>curve</em> in \(\lambda\) and differentiate its optimality condition. Stationarity \(\nabla_x g(x^*(\lambda);\lambda) = 0\) holds identically in \(\lambda\), so differentiating through gives a predictor for how the optimum moves,</p>
  <p>\[ H(\lambda)\, \frac{dx^*}{d\lambda} + \big(\nabla f_1 - \nabla f_2\big) = 0, \qquad H(\lambda) = \lambda \nabla^2 f_1 + (1-\lambda)\nabla^2 f_2, \]</p>
  <p>so that</p>
  <p>\[ \frac{dx^*}{d\lambda} = -H(\lambda)^{-1}\big(\nabla f_1(x^*) - \nabla f_2(x^*)\big). \]</p>
  <p>Numerically integrating this ODE <em>traces</em> the Pareto frontier as a continuous object, at the cost of gradients and a Hessian solve per step instead of a fresh optimization per weight.</p>

  <h3>Where the subspace enters</h3>
  <p>The tracing step needs \(H(\lambda)^{-1}\), which is exactly where a large \(m\) hurts. Each criterion carries its own active subspace, obtained from the eigendecomposition of its average gradient outer product,</p>
  <p>\[ C_i = \int_X \nabla f_i(x)\, \nabla f_i(x)^\top \rho(x)\, dx = W_i \Lambda_i W_i^\top, \quad i = 1,2. \]</p>
  <p>The two criteria do not agree on which directions matter, so neither subspace will serve on its own &mdash; a direction inactive for one may well be active for the other. Rather than stacking the two bases, the aggregate is found <em>geometrically</em>. The dominant \(r\)-dimensional eigenspaces are points \([W_1],[W_2] \in \mathrm{Gr}(r,m)\), and the geodesic between them</p>
  <p>\[ [U(t)] = \exp_{[W_1]}\!\big(t \log_{[W_1]}[W_2]\big), \qquad t \in [0,1], \]</p>
  <p>sweeps a one-parameter family of candidate subspaces running from one criterion's answer to the other's. A scalar sub-optimization then selects the point along that geodesic which <em>balances</em> how well the shared subspace explains each criterion, measured by the coefficient of determination \(R^2_i(t)\) of the corresponding ridge approximation,</p>
  <p>\[ t^\star = \underset{t \in [0,1]}{\arg\min}\ \big|\, R^2_1(t) - R^2_2(t) \,\big|, \qquad U := U(t^\star). \]</p>
  <p>Tracing then runs in the reduced coordinates \(y = U^\top x\), so the Hessian solve is \(r \times r\) rather than \(m \times m\). Choosing \(r = 2\) additionally buys explainability: the entire trade-off becomes something you can draw and inspect, rather than a black-box optimizer output. Note this is the same Grassmannian machinery as the shape work above &mdash; here the geodesic interpolates between two notions of <em>which directions matter</em>, rather than between two shapes.</p>

